---
/**
 * The hero: a real drawing and the real render made from it.
 *
 * ── Why this replaced the drawn placeholder ─────────────────────────────────
 * The previous version was an inline SVG illustration, and its own comment said
 * why: "until there is a real capture to put here". That condition is now met —
 * the render pipeline has produced finished Cycles frames of a real villa, and
 * the plan reader has produced the storey drawings they came from.
 *
 * Showing an illustration was the worse failure than a placeholder box. A
 * visualisation company whose largest on-page element is clip art is making a
 * claim about its own output quality, and the claim is wrong. The product's
 * actual frames are better than anything drawn to stand in for them.
 *
 * ── Why the PAIR, and not just the pretty render ────────────────────────────
 * A render alone says "we make images", which every competitor also says and
 * which a stock photo could fake. The plan beside it says the thing that is
 * actually hard: this image was derived from that drawing. The transformation
 * IS the product, so the transformation is what the hero shows.
 *
 * ── Performance ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
 * `fetchpriority="high"` and no lazy-loading on the render: it is the LCP
 * element, and lazy-loading the thing the page is about delays the only paint
 * that matters. The plan inset IS lazy — it is decorative-adjacent and below
 * the fold on narrow screens. Both carry intrinsic width/height so nothing
 * reflows when they land.
 */
---

<div class="mx-auto mt-14 w-full max-w-5xl">
  <figure class="relative">
    <!-- The render, in a browser frame: it is a published, shareable page,
         not a picture file emailed to a client. -->
    <div class="overflow-hidden rounded-card border border-line bg-surface shadow-lg">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-2 border-b border-line bg-surface-alt px-4 py-3">
        <span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-full bg-line"></span>
        <span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-full bg-line"></span>
        <span class="h-2.5 w-2.5 rounded-full bg-line"></span>
        <span
          class="ml-3 hidden truncate rounded-full bg-surface px-3 py-1 text-xs text-ink-soft sm:block"
        >
          arcvia.com/w/villa-a &middot; living room
        </span>
      </div>

      <img
        src="/hero/interior.webp"
        width="1920"
        height="1080"
        alt="An interior walkthrough view of a villa living room, rendered by Arcvia from the architect's floor plan"
        class="block aspect-video w-full object-cover"
        fetchpriority="high"
        decoding="async"
      />
    </div>

    <!-- The source drawing. Deliberately small and offset: it is the input, and
         the eye should reach it second. -->
    <div
      class="absolute -bottom-6 -left-2 hidden w-32 overflow-hidden rounded-card border border-line bg-surface shadow-lg sm:block md:-left-8 md:w-44"
    >
      <img
        src="/hero/plan.webp"
        width="800"
        height="1440"
        alt="The architect's first-floor plan that this walkthrough was built from"
        class="block w-full object-cover"
        style="aspect-ratio: 800 / 1440"
        loading="lazy"
        decoding="async"
      />
      <figcaption
        class="border-t border-line px-2 py-1.5 text-center text-[10px] font-medium uppercase tracking-wider text-ink-soft"
      >
        The drawing
      </figcaption>
    </div>
  </figure>

  <p class="mt-10 text-center text-sm text-ink-soft sm:mt-8">
    A real project. The drawing on the left became the walkthrough on the right &mdash;
    no remodelling, no 3D artist.
  </p>
</div>
